## A journey shaped by the goddesses.

Most trips through Upper Egypt follow the kings: the tombs, the colossi, the names carved deep into the stone. This one follows the goddesses. Mut, the mother of Thebes. Hathor, goddess of love, music and joy. Isis, who gathered what was broken and made it whole. Satet, who guarded the southern border and the coming of the flood. Their temples run the length of the river from Dendera to the First Cataract, and most visitors see only one or two of them, in a hurry, between other things.

We built this route for a guest who asked for something different: fewer sites, more time, and the quiet to feel each place rather than tick it off. Four days, one concierge, and the goddesses in the order the river gives them.

## Day by day

**Day 1, Karnak and the Precinct of Mut.** Karnak first, then the walk south to Mut's precinct, where lion-headed Sekhmet sits in rows beside a crescent sacred lake and you will often be alone. A gentle first day, with time to rest.

**Day 2, the goddesses of Luxor's West Bank.** Hathor's small Ptolemaic temple above the tomb-builders' village at Deir el-Medina, the hidden Isis temple of Deir el-Shelwit at the desert's edge, and Hatshepsut's terraces, where Hathor had her shrine. A felucca at sunset to close it.

**Day 3, Hathor and Osiris.** North to Dendera: Hathor's face on every column, the blue painted ceilings, the stairways up to the roof chapels. Then Abydos, the holy ground of Osiris, Seti I's temple and the Osireion seen from above. Back in Luxor for the evening.

**Day 4, south to Isis.** With your luggage, the road south to Aswan, with Kom Ombo on the way (or El Kab, the town of the vulture goddess Nekhbet, if you prefer). Philae by private boat in the afternoon light, and your night in Aswan.

**The last morning, Satet.** By boat to Elephantine, to Satet's sanctuary at Egypt's old frontier, before your concierge takes you to your hotel or the airport.

> Each of them was a different answer to the same question: what keeps life going? Four days is just enough time to listen to all four.

## One person beside you, the whole way

Your concierge collects you every morning and stays with you between every temple, every boat and every table, and knows when to explain and when to say nothing. How the days are timed and routed is our craft, and local knowledge built over years; all you need to know is that it is taken care of. On the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you; if you'd rather it was only the two of you, that works too.

Every ticket and every boat is settled before you land. Your nights are yours to choose: your concierge sends you a few trusted places in Luxor and Aswan, and you book them directly.

## Who it's for

Travellers who have read about these goddesses and want to stand in their houses. Women marking a milestone. Anyone who would rather spend a long time in four sacred places than a short time in fourteen. Tell us what draws you, and we'll shape the four days around it.
